## Step 1: Get the Frontend Files
Ask your team member to provide their frontend files (HTML, CSS, JavaScript, React build, etc.)

## Step 2: Place Frontend Files
Put the frontend files in the `public` folder I created:

```
your-project/
├── public/ ← Frontend files go here
│ ├── index.html
│ ├── css/
│ ├── js/
│ └── assets/
├── src/
│ └── enhanced-server.js
└── run-enhanced-server.bat
```

## Step 3: Frontend API Integration
Your team member needs to configure their frontend to call your API endpoints:

**Base URL for API calls:**
```javascript
const API_BASE_URL = 'http://localhost:9000/api';
```

**Example API calls from frontend:**
```javascript
// Get company data
fetch(`${API_BASE_URL}/companies/Apple`)
 .then(response => response.json())
 .then(data => console.log(data));

// Compare companies
fetch(`${API_BASE_URL}/comparison?company1=Apple&company2=Microsoft`)
 .then(response => response.json())
 .then(data => console.log(data));
```

## Step 4: Start the Server
Run your server as usual:
```batch
run-enhanced-server.bat
```

The server will now serve:
- Frontend at `http://localhost:9000/`
- API at `http://localhost:9000/api/`

## Alternative: Keep Separate Servers
If the frontend runs on its own server (like React dev server on port 3000), it can still call your API at `http://localhost:9000/api/` - CORS is already enabled.

Which approach does your team member prefer?"}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<additional_data>
