## Option 1: Network Access (Recommended)
Your server already supports network access. When you run `run-enhanced-server.bat`, it shows:

```
✓ Local: http://localhost:9000/
✓ On Your Network: http://192.168.x.x:9000/
```

Your team member can call your API from their separate frontend using your network IP:

```javascript
// From their frontend code
const API_BASE_URL = 'http://YOUR_IP:9000/api';

fetch(`${API_BASE_URL}/companies/Apple`)
 .then(response => response.json())
 .then(data => console.log(data));
```

## Option 2: Deploy to Cloud
Deploy your backend to a cloud service:

**Free options:**
- **Railway** - Easy Node.js deployment
- **Render** - Free tier available
- **Cyclic** - Simple deployment
- **Fly.io** - Free allowance

Then your API becomes accessible at:
```
https://your-app-name.railway.app/api/
```

## Option 3: Tunneling (Quick Testing)
Use ngrok to expose your local server:

```bash
# Install ngrok
# Then run:
ngrok http 9000
```

This gives you a public URL like `https://abc123.ngrok.io` that forwards to your local server.

## What Your Team Member Needs:
Just the API documentation (already in your `FRONTEND_GUIDE.md`) and your server's URL.

**Which option works best for your team?** Network access is easiest if you're on the same network, cloud deployment is best for production."}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<additional_data>
