# https://medium.com/@vedrathi10/organizing-s-p-500-companies-by-sector-9464def38cca
# Webscraping &amp; Using API to Collect Data and Organize S&amp;P 500 Companies | by Vedant Rathi | Medium

# Webscraping & Using API to Collect Data and Organize S&P 500 Companies
[](/@vedrathi10?source=post_page---byline--9464def38cca---------------------------------------)[Vedant Rathi](/@vedrathi10?source=post_page---byline--9464def38cca---------------------------------------)Follow4 min read·Jul 5, 2023[
](/m/signin?actionUrl=https%3A%2F%2Fmedium.com%2F_%2Fvote%2Fp%2F9464def38cca&operation=register&redirect=https%3A%2F%2Fmedium.com%2F%40vedrathi10%2Forganizing-s-p-500-companies-by-sector-9464def38cca&user=Vedant+Rathi&userId=ff9dad27bfe0&source=---header_actions--9464def38cca---------------------clap_footer------------------)--

[](/m/signin?actionUrl=https%3A%2F%2Fmedium.com%2F_%2Fbookmark%2Fp%2F9464def38cca&operation=register&redirect=https%3A%2F%2Fmedium.com%2F%40vedrathi10%2Forganizing-s-p-500-companies-by-sector-9464def38cca&source=---header_actions--9464def38cca---------------------bookmark_footer------------------)Listen

Share

In this article, we will explore how we can use Python web scraping and data analysis to show the total market capitalizations of S&P 500 companies sorted by their sector. For up-to-date S&P 500 company information that I will use in the code, I referred to [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_S%26P_500_companies](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_S%26P_500_companies).

Link to code: [https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1_PRv-4UWca9-UxrRSVP8gy-69gvlPEyO?usp=sharing](https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1_PRv-4UWca9-UxrRSVP8gy-69gvlPEyO?usp=sharing)

!pip install requests beautifulsoup4import pandas as pdimport numpy as npfrom bs4 import BeautifulSoupimport matplotlib.pyplot as pltfrom matplotlib.ticker import FuncFormatterimport requestsimport yfinance as yfWe first start by installing/importing the libraries we will be using in the Python code. BeautifulSoup is a powerful library that enables one to extract information from websites with just a few lines of code.

url = &#x27;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_S%26P_500_companies&#x27;response = requests.get(url)soup = BeautifulSoup(response.text, &#x27;html.parser&#x27;)table = soup.find(&#x27;tbody&#x27;)data = []rows = table.find_all(&#x27;tr&#x27;)for row in rows: cells = row.find_all(&#x27;td&#x27;) row_data = [cell.get_text(strip=True) for cell in cells] data.append(row_data)dfSP = pd.DataFrame(data)columns = [&#x27;Symbol&#x27;, &#x27;Security&#x27;, &#x27;GICS Sector&#x27;, &#x27;GICS Sub-Industry&#x27;, &#x27;Headquarters Location&#x27;, &#x27;Date Added&#x27;, &#x27;CIK&#x27;, &#x27;Founded&#x27;]dfSP.columns = columnsdfSP = dfSP.iloc[1:]print(dfSP.head())With BeautifulSoup, we can enter a URL and parse through the HTML content of the URL. In our case, we parse through a table in the Wikipedia page that we wish to copy over into a data frame. The <tbody> HTML element represents the entire table, <tr> are the table rows, and the <td> elements are the data inside the rows. Hence, we populate our data frame with these values and then rename the columns exactly as Wikipedia has it. The reason the Wikipedia page has 503 rows (at the time I accessed it), instead of 500, is because three of the companies, namely Alphabet Inc., Fox Corporation, and News Corp, have two share classes of stocks.

dfSP[&#x27;Market Capitalization&#x27;] = NonedfSP[&#x27;Free Cash Flow&#x27;] = NonedfSP[&#x27;Total Debt&#x27;] = Nonefor index, row in dfSP.iterrows(): symbol = row[&#x27;Symbol&#x27;] ticker = yf.Ticker(symbol) info = ticker.info market_cap = info.get(&#x27;marketCap&#x27;, None) free_cash_flow = info.get(&#x27;freeCashflow&#x27;, None) total_debt = info.get(&#x27;totalDebt&#x27;, None) dfSP.at[index, &#x27;Market Capitalization&#x27;] = market_cap dfSP.at[index, &#x27;Free Cash Flow&#x27;] = free_cash_flow dfSP.at[index, &#x27;Total Debt&#x27;] = total_debtdfSP[&#x27;Market Capitalization&#x27;] = pd.to_numeric(dfSP[&#x27;Market Capitalization&#x27;])print(dfSP.head())Now we use the Yahoo Finance API to access all the S&P 500 company’s (1) market caps — the total value of all of a company’s outstanding shares, (2) total debts, and (3) free cash flows — the leftover cash companies have after taking into account cash outflows, and populate this into a new column in our data frame. It may take up to a few minutes to finish executing as API calls can induce latency especially since we have a data frame with hundreds of rows.

def plot_bar_chart(df, column, title, y_label, formatter, save_path): sector_unique = df[&#x27;GICS Sector&#x27;].unique() values_by_sector = [] for sector in sector_unique: dfFiltered = df[df[&#x27;GICS Sector&#x27;] == sector] value = dfFiltered[column].sum() values_by_sector.append(value) fig, ax = plt.subplots() ax.bar(sector_unique, values_by_sector) ax.yaxis.set_major_formatter(FuncFormatter(formatter)) ax.set_xlabel(&#x27;GICS Sector&#x27;, fontsize=12) ax.set_ylabel(y_label) ax.set_title(title) plt.xticks(rotation=90, fontsize=8) plt.show() plt.close()columns = [&#x27;Market Capitalization&#x27;, &#x27;Total Debt&#x27;, &#x27;Free Cash Flow&#x27;]titles = [&#x27;Total Market Cap by GICS Sector&#x27;, &#x27;Total Debt by GICS Sector&#x27;, &#x27;Free Cash Flow by GICS Sector&#x27;]y_labels = [&#x27;Total Market Cap (in Trillions)&#x27;, &#x27;Total Debt (in Trillions)&#x27;, &#x27;Free Cash Flow (in Billions)&#x27;]formatters = [ lambda x, pos: f&#x27;{x * 1e-12:.1f}T&#x27;, lambda x, pos: f&#x27;{x * 1e-12:.1f}T&#x27;, lambda x, pos: f&#x27;{x * 1e-9:.2f} billion&#x27;]for i, column in enumerate(columns): plot_bar_chart(dfSP, column, titles[i], y_labels[i], formatters[i], save_path=None)Next, we create a function that calculates the sum of the values categorized by the GICS sector for each of the columns representing market cap, total debt, and free cash flows. We use many techniques to do so, including data frame filtering and using built-in Python methods. I also added formatting so the y-axis values will be in terms of billions or trillions, making it look neater visually. Depending on when you run this code, the charts will look different as these values and the composition of the S&P 500 are always changing.

sectors = dfSP[&#x27;GICS Sector&#x27;].unique()dfCategory = pd.DataFrame(columns=[&#x27;Market Capitalization&#x27;, &#x27;Free Cash Flow&#x27;, &#x27;Total Debt&#x27;])for sector in sectors: dfFiltered = dfSP[dfSP[&#x27;GICS Sector&#x27;] == sector] totals = dfFiltered[[&#x27;Market Capitalization&#x27;, &#x27;Free Cash Flow&#x27;, &#x27;Total Debt&#x27;]].sum() dfCategory = pd.concat([dfCategory, totals.to_frame().transpose()], ignore_index=True)dfCategory[&#x27;Sector&#x27;] = sectorsdfCategory.set_index(&#x27;Sector&#x27;, inplace=True)def format_trillions(value): return f&#x27;{value * 1e-12:.2f} trillion&#x27;def format_billions(value): return f&#x27;{value * 1e-9:.2f} billion&#x27;dfCategory[&#x27;Market Capitalization&#x27;] = dfCategory[&#x27;Market Capitalization&#x27;].apply(format_trillions)dfCategory[&#x27;Total Debt&#x27;] = dfCategory[&#x27;Total Debt&#x27;].apply(format_trillions)dfCategory[&#x27;Free Cash Flow&#x27;] = dfCategory[&#x27;Free Cash Flow&#x27;].apply(format_billions)dfCategoryFinally, we reorganize the bar chart into a data frame, with three columns representing market caps, total debt, and free cash flow. It categorizes all the numbers in the charts into a table format. Instead of adding all the values by column, we add the rows to all of the columns simultaneously as it is more efficient and clean.
"},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You've got a great idea for improving the user experience! Adding a searchable dropdown with a comprehensive list of company names would indeed reduce input errors and make the tool much easier to use.

However, implementing a dropdown with over 10,000 company names directly in the current HTML structure (which is a simple server-side generated page) presents a few challenges:

1. **Data Acquisition**:
 * CompaniesMarketCap.com, as per their `/stockmarket-api/` page, **does not offer a public API** to directly fetch their full list of companies. They list some partner APIs, but these are generally paid services and would require significant integration effort.
 * Scraping 10,000+ company names from their website to populate a dropdown would be:
 * **Fragile**: Web scrapers break easily if the website structure changes.
 * **Slow**: Fetching and parsing that much data on every page load or even on server start would be inefficient.
 * **Potentially Against Terms of Service**: Many websites prohibit extensive scraping.

2. **Frontend Performance**:
 * Loading 10,000+ items into a simple HTML `<select>` dropdown can make the webpage very slow and unresponsive.
 * The browser would struggle to render and manage such a large list efficiently without a more advanced frontend solution.

3. **Current Architecture**:
 * The `enhanced-server.js` primarily serves HTML generated on the server side. Implementing a dynamic, searchable dropdown with that many items typically requires client-side JavaScript and a more robust frontend framework/library (like React, Vue, Angular, or even just a dedicated autocomplete library like Select2 or Awesomplete).

**Recommended Approach (Considering Current Limitations):**

Given the current setup, a full 10,000+ company dropdown sourced directly and dynamically from CompaniesMarketCap isn't immediately feasible without a significant architectural shift or a reliable API source.

**Alternative and More Feasible Improvements (Sticking to a Similar Tech Stack):**

1. **Enhanced Server-Side Symbol Search (What we've been working on)**:
 * Continue refining the `searchStockSymbol` function using Alpha Vantage (or another free, reliable symbol search API if one exists).
 * The goal is to make it very good at translating a user-typed company name (even with slight variations) into the correct stock ticker. This is the most crucial part for data accuracy.

2. **"Did You Mean?" Feature (Server-Side)**:
 * If `searchStockSymbol` returns multiple good matches, instead of picking one, the server could render a page saying: "Did you mean: \[Option A (Symbol: AAA)], \[Option B (Symbol: BBB)]?" with clickable links.

3. **Cached List of Popular Companies (Compromise)**:
 * Manually or semi-automatically curate a list of, say, the top 200-500 most searched/popular companies.
 * Store this list in a simple JSON file within your project.
 * Load this smaller list into a standard HTML `<datalist>` element associated with the input fields. This provides autocomplete suggestions for common companies without overwhelming the browser.
 ```html
 <input type="text" id="company1" name="company1" list="company-suggestions">
 <datalist id="company-suggestions">
 <option value="Apple">
 <option value="Microsoft">
 <option value="Google">
 <!-- ... more options ... -->
 </datalist>
 ```
 * This wouldn't be exhaustive but would cover many common use cases and reduce typos for popular stocks.

4. **Focus on Robust Fallback for "Company Not Found"**:
 * If a company name cannot be resolved to a symbol by any means, provide a very clear message to the user, e.g., "Company 'XYZ' not found. Please check the spelling or try a different name. Ensure you are using the official company name."

**If a Full Dynamic Dropdown is a Must-Have:**

This would likely require:
* Finding a **reliable API** that provides a list of all companies and their symbols (this is often a paid feature).
* Or, periodically running a **scraper script** (separate from the main server) to update a local JSON file with company names from a source like Wikipedia's S&P 500 list or other public listings. This list would still be large.
* Implementing an **autocomplete/typeahead JavaScript library** on the frontend to efficiently search and display suggestions from this large local JSON list without lagging the browser. This means moving some UI logic to client-side JavaScript.

For now, I recommend we continue to focus on making the existing name-to-symbol resolution as robust as possible and perhaps implement the `<datalist>` with popular companies as a quick enhancement.

